course: dictionary
lesson: 6911
title: Words from science
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words come from science, and you will meet them in lots of places. Oxygen is a noun, and it is uncountable: you say some oxygen, not an oxygen. It is a gas in the air that people, animals and plants need to live.
explain: Read the words around it for clues: «When you breathe in, air goes down your windpipe into two lungs, which are full of tiny air bags; there oxygen passes into the blood.» Breathe in, lungs and blood tell you that oxygen is something your body takes in to keep going.
explain: Oxygen is also a chemical element, with the symbol O. Water is made of hydrogen and oxygen, so scientists write it as H2O. The same word can have more than one meaning, so the context tells you which one the writer means.
fact: Oxygen is an uncountable noun: you say some oxygen, not two oxygens.
fact: «Oxygen» means a gas in the air that people, animals and plants need to live.
fact: As a chemical element, oxygen has the symbol O.
fact: About one fifth of the air around you is oxygen.
fact: Plants give off oxygen when they make food using sunlight.
exercise: «Breathed-out air contains less oxygen and more carbon dioxide than breathed-in air.» Which gas is in breathed-out air in a larger amount? | *Carbon dioxide | Water | Oxygen | Blood
exercise: Which letter is the chemical symbol for oxygen? | G | *O | W | H
exercise: In «A frog can take in some oxygen through its thin, moist skin», why is there no s or a on oxygen? | Oxygen is always plural | *Oxygen is uncountable, so it has no plural | Oxygen is a verb, so it never changes | Oxygen is a name
test: «Walking sharks can survive for hours with very little oxygen when a rock pool gets warm and stale at low tide.» What does very little oxygen mean? | Very cold water | *Only a small amount of the gas | Lots of bubbles in the water | No water at all
test: When you breathe in, oxygen passes from the lungs into which part of the body? | The stomach | Hair | Bones | *The blood
exam: «Fire needs oxygen to burn.» What would happen to a fire in a sealed jar with no oxygen left? | It would turn blue | *It would go out | It would melt the jar | It would grow bigger and brighter
